use @piton/belay

from ./lib/CruxUiBase import CruxUiBase

export skill CruxUiDevelop extends CruxUiBase:
    description:
        Develops one part of a user interface through a short adaptive
        interview and rounds of saved, numbered mockup variations shown
        on one gallery page, then records the user's pick and what it
        settled in the Piton specification.
    useWhen:
        the user wants to prototype, design, or explore a screen, page,
        component, flow, or interaction, wants to see UI options before
        deciding, or wants to develop the UI part of a specification
    prompt:
        Your goal is to turn a rough idea of a UI into a specified,
        illustrated part of the project. Work in two kinds of step. An
        interview finds out which part of the UI to work on and how it
        should work. Mockup rounds show options the user can see and
        pick from. The specification and its saved mockups are the
        result. Never write application code.

        First find the project and its UI. Read the specification for
        the parts that describe the UI, such as surfaces, pages,
        components, flows, navigation, design rules, and shape
        instructions, and the domain data those parts show. Note what
        is already decided, what mockups already exist, and which
        rounds are unfinished.

        Then establish the target, the one part of the UI to work on.
        If the user named it, use it. If not, offer a short list of
        candidates by what they are, such as the restaurant card or
        the search flow, never by file name, and leave room for
        something else. Choosing the target does not count as a
        question. If the target is too broad for one set of mockups,
        such as the whole app, suggest a narrower part first, and let
        the user keep the broad target if they want.

    interview:
        Before your own questions, ask how many questions the user
        wants to answer, with 5 as the default. Accepting the default,
        any other positive number, or no questions at all is fine. This
        choice does not count as a question. Then ask exactly one
        question per turn, show progress such as Question 2 of 5, and
        keep to the budget exactly. Never ask what the project or an
        earlier answer already settles. Clarifications and examples
        about the same question do not use up another one.

        Spend the questions on what most changes the UI, chosen from
        everything learned so far, never from a fixed list. Useful
        areas include who uses this part and what they are trying to
        do, what it must show and in what order of importance, what the
        user can do and what happens when they do it, where it sits in
        the flow and how people arrive and leave, which states matter,
        such as empty, loading, error, or a long list, which devices
        and screen sizes matter, and any look and feel or examples the
        user likes or dislikes. A question may offer options or a
        recommendation. When the user does not know, keep the point
        open and let a mockup round explore it.

        When the budget is spent, write what was learned into the
        specification before the first round. Update the anchor that
        describes the target, or create one where the project's
        conventions say it belongs. Keep confirmed decisions apart
        from open questions, and record open questions as such rather
        than guessing. Validate with the Piton tools. Then start a
        mockup round.

    mockupRounds:
        At the start of every round, ask how many variations to make,
        with 3 as the default. This does not count as a question. Name
        the question the round explores in one sentence, such as how
        the card shows opening hours, and choose it from the open
        questions with the biggest effect on the UI. The first round
        usually explores the overall layout. Later rounds start from
        the last pick and explore what it left open, or the change the
        user asked for.

        Make exactly the number of variations asked for, save them and
        the round's gallery, update the top-level mockups index, and
        show the gallery. Then ask the user which number they like.
        Say they can pick one, combine parts of several, ask for
        changes, dive deeper into one, or reject all of them.

        Diving deeper into one variation starts a new round built only
        on that variation, and the user again chooses how many
        variations to make. Rejecting all of them starts a new round
        that explores different directions, and you ask what was wrong
        before making it, as a clarification that is not a question
        from the budget.

        After each pick, record it as the shared rules say, validate,
        and say in one or two lines what the pick settled. Then offer
        three options. Good enough ends the session. Another round
        refines the pick or explores the next open question. More
        questions starts a new interview with a new budget, default 5.
        Do not move on to building the UI or to any other workflow
        on your own.

    resuming:
        Keep enough state to resume an interrupted session without
        storing the conversation. The specification holds the
        decisions, the open questions and the mockups property. The
        mockups folder holds every round and its pick. On resume, read
        both before asking anything. A round with a gallery but no pick
        is waiting for the user to choose, so show it again and ask.
        Never repeat an answered question and never redo a finished
        round.

    boundaries:
        This skill decides how one part of the UI looks and behaves and
        records it. When the work turns into developing a feature that
        is not mainly about the UI, recommend crux-spec-develop. When an
        existing UI specification needs more detail without mockups,
        recommend crux-spec-deep-dive. When the user wants to build the
        picked design, say that this skill stops at the specification
        and its mockups.
